# `power::EstimateResult`

The estimated state, residuals, covariance and diagnostics.

## Signature

```cpp
power::EstimateResult
```

## What It Does

The result of `estimate`. Arrays keep the leading scenario axes.

## Fields

| Field | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `voltage` | Complex estimated bus voltages, `[..., bus]`. |  |
| `magnitude` | Estimated voltage magnitudes. |  |
| `angle` | Estimated voltage angles in radians. |  |
| `injection` | The complex bus injections implied by the estimate, `[..., bus]`. |  |
| `from_power` | Complex power entering each line at its sending end, `[..., line]`. |  |
| `to_power` | Complex power entering each line at its receiving end. |  |
| `predicted` | `h(x)` at the estimate, `[..., measurement]`, in the order given. |  |
| `residual` | `value - predicted`. |  |
| `normalized_residual` | The residual divided by its own standard deviation: large values flag suspect measurements. |  |
| `objective` | The minimum weighted sum of squares `J`. |  |
| `covariance` | The covariance of the estimated state, `[..., state, state]`, the angles of the non-reference buses first and then the magnitudes. |  |
| `converged` | True when the iteration converged, `[...]`. |  |
| `iterations` | Gauss-Newton iterations used. |  |

## Returns

Returned by `estimate`.

## Errors

Reading a field does not solve again.

## Interpretation and Use

Compare `objective` with a chi-square threshold of the redundancy and read the normalised residuals. The estimator reports this evidence; it does not remove measurements.

